            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The palm (kaph) primarily symbolizes God's intimate care, protective remembrance (being engraved on God's palms), and security. Secondary meanings include prayer (uplifted palms), generosity, and sometimes general hand imagery like power and action.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The palm (kaph) specifically represents the 'hand laid open'—functioning to cover, carry, or offer—and is distinct from the hand of power (yad).\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Divine care, protective remembrance, prayer, and sometimes power or action.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The open hand used to cover, carry, or offer (excluding power).\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book explicitly excludes 'power' from the symbolism of kaph, assigning it instead to yad. The consensus sometimes includes power, agency, or action as associated with kaph.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. The book identifies a unifying physical trait (the laid-open hand) for the consensus's recognized themes of protection, remembrance, and offering, while removing the broader associations of power.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book refines the consensus by distinguishing kaph from yad based on physical function (the open palm vs. the forceful hand), unifying the accepted meanings of covering (protection), carrying (remembrance), and offering (generosity) while filtering out power.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"Some biblical passages may use kaph in contexts of striking or grasping that overlap with concepts of power, which could challenge a strict dichotomy between kaph (open hand) and yad (power).\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
